WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:07.459
I want to do a story about this idea that
an ounce of prevention is really worth a pound

00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:08.459
of cure.

00:00:08.459 --> 00:00:13.589
There are some states that did not take coronavirus
seriously early on.

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:18.760
And those states are the ones that now are
having some of the largest outbreaks in the

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.760
world.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.449
On a case growth rate basis and some of them
are being forced to now go in the complete

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:32.619
opposite direction and adopt some of the even
more authoritarian techniques or rules than

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.420
we saw in some of the early hotspots.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.410
And we talked earlier about California, Florida,
Arizona, Carolinas, Tennessee, Louisiana,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.829
Mississippi, you know, red states, plus California,
basically.

00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:47.579
But I want to talk about Texas briefly right
now.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:51.350
Texas hesitated to do much early on in the
pandemic.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:56.480
Texas then after sort of closing some stuff,
they started reopening very quickly.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.730
Mask use was not high in Texas for a livery
longtime there.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.660
Republican Lieutenant Governor Dan Patrick.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.380
You might remember, went on TV and said, I
would sacrifice myself.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:12.480
And I know many other grandparents would as
well to corona virus to save the economy.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.150
But things have gotten so ugly so quickly
in Texas.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:22.740
I see use very full hospitals, very full approaching
what I'm calling virus escape velocity in

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:29.170
Texas, that Hidalgo County, Texas, issued
a threat that a notice, I guess I would call

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:35.960
it that if you test positive for the virus
and you break the mandatory 14 day quarantine,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.140
you will be prosecuted criminally.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.580
The Hidalgo County Twitter account tweeted
this out, making it clear, do not leave your

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:43.580
house.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:44.750
Do not have any visitors.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:50.500
Make a list of people that you have been in
contact with and you are subject to criminal

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.420
prosecution if you don't comply with this.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.870
Now, there's a few things here, of course,
to think about.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.370
One is, are they really going to prosecute?

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:58.420
I don't know.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.940
They're certainly threatening to.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.510
Now, of course, a lot of Texas right wingers
are saying things like this is the Gestapo.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.930
These are jackbooted government thugs.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.540
The truth is actually the opposite.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:16.920
If a place needs to do something like this,
it's because people are not doing it already

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.930
in any common, sensible world.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:24.640
You would never need such a notice because
obviously anybody who test positive would

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.810
not be leaving their house.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:30.340
They would be home for 14 days because they
understand the risk of continuing to spread

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.340
the disease.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:38.140
The fact that this was published is a signal
about how bad the situation is.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.930
And meanwhile, if we zoom out from Hidalgo
County to Texas more broadly, the death curve

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.810
is looking very, very ugly.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:50.140
A very steep spike in the seven day moving
average of death numbers in Texas.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.940
No sign that things are turning around.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:57.441
Remember, we have a progression, which is
cases spike, hospitalizations, spike, and

00:02:57.441 --> 00:03:02.630
then deaths spike early in the case, spike
in sort of like mid to late June.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:07.790
There were a lot of deniers, I would call
them, who were just repeating cases aren't

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:08.970
going to lead to deaths.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.790
It's younger people getting it.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.569
We are better at treating it.

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:13.570
It's less serious now.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:17.090
We know all these different Compaq pick your
poison, so to speak.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.720
And I was interested in that.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:24.430
And I came to you and I said, this may be
true or it may just be the 20 day lag time.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.000
Then we started to see hospitalizations increase
in Texas.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.730
We heard in Houston that a bunch of icey used
were getting full.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.460
We heard in Austin, the mayor of Austin was
concerned about how full they were getting.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.520
That was the sign that this may just be a
lag time.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:41.500
It may be, as we've seen, cases followed by
hospitalizations, followed by deaths and case

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:42.750
in point.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:43.930
That is what we are seeing right now.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:48.881
Now, the big hot spots in the U.S., again,
reminder, it's basically California and a

00:03:48.881 --> 00:03:50.480
bunch of red states.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.500
Dr. Foushee a couple of weeks ago said we
could be heading towards 100000 cases per

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:57.590
day.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:03.310
We've been sort of steadying on weekdays between
60 and 75000 cases per day.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:08.750
I wouldn't be shocked if we do an eighty thousand
new case day this week if things do not turn

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:10.230
around again.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.620
I know I'm doing this twice today, but it's
so important.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.000
I know we have a lot of viewers in these in
these areas.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.430
We are going to reach virus escape velocity.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.150
And by August 15th, it could make today look,
not that bad.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.500
And that's very, very scary.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:27.720
So we haven't won.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.330
This is not what winning looks like.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.430
Do not be confused.

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:35.720
Not that many of you are, but most of you
understand that we are not winning.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.360
And forget about August 15th.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.970
What's the fall going to look like if we never
flatten the curve?

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:48.260
Remember, we were expecting a total dip to
almost no new cases, almost no new deaths

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.050
by June 1st or June 15th.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:51.050
It never happened.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:56.540
We had a slight decline, down to 20000 cases
a day and then back up like a rocket.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:17.760
What will the fall look like

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:51.260
if

00:05:51.260 --> 00:06:16.699
this doesn't turn around?

